# The stall diagnostic: an app thread that stops taking its occurrences
# is REPORTED, not merely survived.
#
# This is the one scene that deliberately misuses kaya. Every other
# guest is careful to keep blocking work off the app thread — the
# filedialog guests each carry a paragraph explaining why they hand the
# read to a worker — and nothing proved that kaya would notice a guest
# that did not. DESIGN's threading section promised it would.
#
# WHY TWO CLICKS. The consumer cursor advances BEFORE a record reaches
# the guest, so a handler blocking on an empty ring is indistinguishable
# from an idle app, and rightly so: nothing is waiting on it. The second
# click is what makes work PENDING while the app thread is gone, which
# is both what the watchdog can see and what a person actually reports —
# they click, and click again, and nothing happens.
#
# AND THE REPORT IS ASSERTED ONLY OF AN ABSENCE THAT NEVER ENDS. A
# BOUNDED absence — the guests' 2500ms `block` — can be caught only
# while it lasts, so asserting it needs the second click to land at
# least KAYA_STALL_MS (1000ms) before the handler returns. That margin
# is the harness's own two round trips through the UI thread, and IT
# GROWS WITH THE MACHINE: measured 2026-08-21 on a loaded emulator it
# reached 2542ms, past the whole block, and the leg failed saying the
# app thread was keeping up — a race against the machine rather than a
# check of the code, and a red nobody believes (docs/traps.md,
# docs/deferred.md). The wedge below has no such margin to lose: the
# click it strands is pending for as long as the assertion needs.
expect label#0 "ready"

# IT GOES AWAY AND IT COMES BACK. The blocked handler returns, the click
# that queued behind it is taken, and the app carries on — so the stall
# the watchdog sees here is a stall and not a death, and nothing was
# dropped while the thread was gone.
click button#0
click button#1
expect label#0 "pinged"

# AND THE WATCHDOG TAKES IT BACK. This is the half nothing asserted for
# four milestones, and its absence shipped a diagnostic that LIED in
# five of the eight languages: the watchdog counted occurrences into the
# transport and counted the ones the CORE handed over, so every guest
# that reads the occurrence ring directly (go, csharp, ocaml, haskell,
# java) reported a stall on every healthy run. A matrix failure then
# arrived carrying "THE APP THREAD IS STALLED" about an app thread that
# was fine, and the session it misdirected is the cost. A diagnostic
# that fires when it should not is worse than none, because it is read
# as evidence — so the scene asserts BOTH directions, and this line is
# the one that fails for a watchdog blind to a transport.
expect_no_stall

# THEN THE ONE THAT NEVER COMES BACK, which is the shape a real deadlock
# has: a handler that blocks for a day. This is where the report is
# asserted, because here the pending click stays pending — the
# assertion can take as long as it likes and still be asking about an
# app thread that is gone.
#
# THE PAUSE KEEPS THE TWO HALVES APART, so the reading this half
# produces is unambiguously its own; expect_no_stall above is what
# actually proves the first one cleared.
settle 300
click button#2
click button#1
expect_stall
# NOTHING FOLLOWS, and nothing can: the app thread never returns. That
# the leg still reports a verdict at all is the other half of what this
# proves — the harness runs on its own thread and asks the MAIN thread
# to exit, so neither path needs the app thread that is wedged.
